<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Product Backlog Management Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Product backlogs can quickly become difficult to manage when teams
                    collect too many requests, lack clear priorities, or fail to regularly
                    refine requirements. Addressing these challenges helps maintain a
                    focused and useful backlog.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Overloaded Backlog:</b> Regularly remove obsolete, duplicate, or low-value items to keep the backlog manageable.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Unclear Requirements:</b> Add sufficient descriptions, acceptance criteria, business context, and expected outcomes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Competing Priorities:</b> Use consistent prioritization criteria to make transparent decisions between different requests.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Large Backlog Items:</b> Break complex requirements into smaller, manageable, and independently deliverable items.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Frequent Priority Changes:</b> Review changes carefully and communicate their impact on roadmap and delivery plans.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Lack of Technical Work:</b> Include technical debt, performance improvements, security work, and infrastructure requirements in the backlog.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Poor Stakeholder Visibility:</b> Maintain clear backlog information so stakeholders understand current priorities and upcoming work.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant challenges image here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Backlog Prioritization and Product Value</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Prioritization is one of the most important aspects of backlog management.
                    Teams need to determine which work provides the greatest value and should
                    receive development attention first. A value-focused backlog helps prevent
                    resources from being spent on low-impact work.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Evaluate customer impact and the number of users affected by each requirement.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Consider revenue, retention, conversion, efficiency, and other business outcomes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Assess development effort and technical complexity before assigning priorities.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Consider dependencies that may affect the order in which work should be delivered.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Balance short-term improvements with long-term strategic product initiatives.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant prioritization image here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Backlog Refinement and User Story Management</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Backlog refinement keeps upcoming work understandable, prioritized,
                    and ready for development. Regular refinement sessions help product
                    and technical teams identify missing information, clarify requirements,
                    and reduce uncertainty before implementation begins.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Review upcoming user stories and requirements with the development team.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Clarify acceptance criteria and expected product behavior.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Split large or complex stories into smaller deliverable units.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Identify missing dependencies, technical constraints, and external requirements.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Estimate effort and confirm that high-priority items are ready for upcoming development cycles.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant refinement image here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

