<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>How to Measure Product Backlog Management Effectiveness</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Measuring backlog management effectiveness helps product teams understand
                    whether their backlog is healthy, prioritized, actionable, and aligned
                    with product objectives. Useful measurements should focus on quality,
                    delivery readiness, and business value.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Backlog Health:</b> Monitor outdated, duplicate, incomplete, or low-value backlog items.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Requirement Readiness:</b> Track how many upcoming items have clear descriptions and acceptance criteria.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Priority Alignment:</b> Evaluate whether delivered work consistently reflects the highest product priorities.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Delivery Predictability:</b> Compare planned backlog work with completed work across development cycles.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Customer Outcomes:</b> Measure whether delivered backlog items improve customer experience, adoption, engagement, or satisfaction.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Business Outcomes:</b> Evaluate revenue, retention, conversion, efficiency, and other expected product results.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant analytics image here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
